A||||8||fieroluke||Ticking noise on 87 2.5||||5||
Z||000000||fieroluke||06-15-2001||10:15 AM||||Hi,<P>my recently rebuilt 87 Iron Duke shows another problem. The rebuild included a new cam, lifters, pushrods, valves and "performance" valve springs. <P>Engine ran great, but after 200 miles, an (old) rocker arm split clean in half (#4 exhaust). No problem. Curse, replace all rockers with new (stock) ones, engine runs great again. <P>Another 300 miles later an (old) rocker arm bolt snaps off right under the bolt head (#4 intake this time). Great. $25 and a set of new rocker arm bolts later, engine runs fine again.<P>Now, another 50 miles later, I get a ticking/clicking noise on startup (at idle). Sounds like one lifter is sticking. It takes about 30 seconds for the noise to get fainter. Then, in "D" under load, it is there again, but disappears as the load disappears. After about 10-20 miles (as the oil warms up), the noise does disappear almost completely, even under load, and you can only hear it if you know what you're listening for. If this was a spun bearing or something, it wouldn't go away with engine/oil temperature, right? And my previous valve train woes make me think in that direction.<P>The Fiero store assures me their springs are only slightly stronger than stock, and nobody had problems before. But I think it takes quite a bit to tear off one of these 10.9 bolts or massive rockers. We did not check the spring rate before installation, because we lack the proper tool, however we assumed since these were new springs, we didn't have to check.<P>We did not need to grind the valve seats much, and the pushrods can be turned by hand if the valve is closed, if that's any help.<P>I do love these little cars, but sometimes you just want to hold a lighter to a fender and feel satisfaction to watch it burn to bits... Unfortunately the engine is unimpressed by threats and curses.<P>Any suggestions, anyone?<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/th_88gt.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||06-15-2001||07:15 PM||||check the exhaust manifold for cracks. The crack can change sizes and thus sound due to heat. The manifold has a habbit of cracking. If you didn't replace it, then it could be part/all of your problem.<P>Exactly what was replaced durring the rebuild. You haven't listed much detail... small things may matter.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||06-15-2001||09:03 PM||||Someone else recently posted valve train problems on a rebuilt 2.5.  As I recall, he was bending pushrods because his valve springs were stacking solid.  Maybe you're having similar problems.<P>Ticking noise that varies directly with engine load?  Yep, could be an exhaust leak.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||06-15-2001||10:53 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>check the exhaust manifold for cracks...The manifold has a habit of cracking...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I noticed my Duke's exhaust manifold is cracked; the "front" end (passenger side) leg of the manifold (i.e. - the no. 1 cylinder's branch) has a crack that goes almost all the way around.<P>Does the cracking tend to occur at that location most of the time?  I'm wondering about a design defect (material, config., tolerance accumulation, or something) that might tend to cause the failures?  Just curious...<P>Oh, and as for the valve train problems:  If you're actually breaking rockers and rocker studs, it sure sounds like some kind of serious interference, doesn't it?  And not when a particular valve is closed, but rather when it's opening!  I'd be tempted to (pull out all the spark plugs and) slowly turn the motor by hand over a few times, before I buttoned everything up.  Sorry if you already tried this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I'm no Duke doctor, either - just offering what I'd probably try, FWIW.       <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000004||theogre||06-15-2001||11:42 PM||||Breaking old rockers and studs on new springs is common enough. It's allong the same kind of problem as putting new lifters on a used cam. Do as sugjested above and check spring clearance as the valve is fully compressed by the rockers.<P>The manifold cracking seems to be a Fiero thing. I've not seen it on FWD cars using the same motor. (In fact those are where I get my replacements... Everyone FWD I've seen is in really good shape.) I expect a combination of water dumping on it and, in automatics, engine vibration. (See the torque strut article in my cave.)<P>If you open the deck wet it will dump everything on the exhaust. With the motor cold... hose the lid then open it and you'll see what I mean. (easier if you watch from side.) the water hits all around the O2 sensor bung. Same area where most cracking shows up.<P>I'm not sure how big a part of this the seal strip may be. I haven't fooled with it. I've had other things to worry about. Not everyone has that. It was suposed to get yanked in recall work. I've been half as_ed trying to work out a good way to make a water deflector that doesn't look like hell but it's not a high priority item.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroluke||06-16-2001||10:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>check the exhaust manifold for cracks. The crack can change sizes and thus sound due to heat. The manifold has a habbit of cracking. If you didn't replace it, then it could be part/all of your problem.<P>Exactly what was replaced durring the rebuild. You haven't listed much detail... small things may matter.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's not ticking like from a manifold (know what that sounds like), it sounds like hitting something with a small, metal hammer.<P>Anyway, here's what we replaced:<BR>Forged Pistons, Chrome-Moly Rings, rod and crank bearings, had crank professionally rebuilt and balanced (ground bearing surfaces to first oversize spec, same goes for block), timing gear, all gaskets, oil pump, water pump, cam,    <BR>lifters, pushrods, rocker arms, valves, valve springs, rocker arm bolts, ignition module, coils, pickup, spark plugs and wires, MAT sensor, O2 sensor, catalytic converter, muffler, battery, oil/air/fuel filters, ultimate dogbone, coolant hoses. Phew. I think that's it, though I may have missed something...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||06-16-2001||12:41 PM||||Go over it with a stephescope or a section of hose and listen to all the diferant areas. that should track it down to what area it's comming from.<P>If it's valves, get some oil stoppers and clip them on the rockers to plug the oil outlets. Then start the car. push on each lifter with a wood block/stick. If one stops the noise you've found the problem. At that point you at least know which valve is batty.<P>You can get the oil stoppers at most decent parts stores. If you run w/o the stoppers oil will fly every where.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DecadenceR||06-16-2001||03:27 PM||||Recently (2 weeks ago) I replaced the springs in my 84 2.5, and I had that kind of sound. For me it did turn out to be the exhaust manifold. But I didn't break anything, so I don't know what to say.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||06-16-2001||09:33 PM||||It not one of those hugely predicatble things. Same with lifters... some times you gets away with it sometimez ya don't...||||reg||1
